  "slug": "phylogenomics-australian-76a6",
  "summary": "Genome-scale and gene-dense datasets are now being used to test discordance, hybridization, and single-origin hypotheses in difficult phylogenies.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is genome-scale data used to resolve relationships where gene trees and organellar or nuclear signals disagree. In Australian Gossypium, whole-genome phylogenomics finds plastid-nuclear discordance but little nuclear evidence of introgression, revisiting homoploid hybrid speciation. In Dipsacales, resequencing of 88 species resolves Linnina backbone relationships, with ILS-driven discordance and possible ancient gene flow. In Aizoaceae, phylotranscriptomics shows gene-dense data resolve major relationships, while short internodes limit resolution within the radiation. In flatfishes, whole-genome phylogenomics and synteny from 17 species support a single origin of body-plan asymmetry. What is new is the emphasis on data density and genomic context, with the Ice Plant result marking limits on what gene-dense data can resolve.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
